     "blocks": [
      {
       "original": {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": "The most common creative phrasing of the theory — \"the photon's journey is time\" — is given its precise content:",
        "level": null,
        "number": null,
        "name": "The most common creative phrasing of the theory — \"the photon's journey is time\" — is given its precise content:"
       },
       "everyone": {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": "The famous poetic line — \"the photon's journey is time\" — gets its precise, honest content here:",
        "level": null,
        "number": null,
        "name": "The famous poetic line — \"the photon's journey is time\" — gets its precise, honest content here:"
       }
      },
      {
       "original": {
        "kind": "list",
        "text": "- Physical ordering: arrival sequence $\\{t_i\\}$ with $t_i = t_{0i} + d_i/c$.\n- Perceptual integration: the system samples this ordered stream at its encoding rate $I(t)$ and integrates over the specious window $W$.\n- Felt rate hypothesis (A5): $\\frac{d\\tau}{dT} = \\Phi[I(t)]$.\n- Everyday illustration: a lightning flash and its thunder — the light arrives nearly instantly; the pressure wave lags ~3 s per km of distance. We perceive the flash \"now\" and the thunder \"now plus delay\"; both are the same event. The *ordering* is physically enforced by carrier speed — and our sense of a temporal gap between flash and thunder is precisely an information-arrival gap, not a physical separation of the event from itself. The cycle reads the same story twice, at two carrier speeds.\n- Astronomical illustration: the Sun is seen as it was 8.3 minutes ago; Andromeda as it was 2.5 million years ago. Star maps are not images of a single \"now\" — they are a montage of *delivered pasts*. The night sky is the cycle's canonical artifact.",
        "level": null,
        "number": null,
        "name": "- Physical ordering: arrival sequence $\\{t_i\\}$ with $t_i = t_{0i} + d_i/c$.\n- Perceptual integration: the system samples this ordered stream at its encoding rate $I(t)$ and integrates over the specious window $W$.\n- Felt rate hypothesis (A5): $\\frac{d\\tau}{dT} = \\Phi[I(t)]$.\n- Everyday illustration: a lightning flash and its thunder — the light arrives nearly instantly; the pressure wave lags ~3 s per km of distance. We perceive the flash \"now\" and the thunder \"now plus delay\"; both are the same event. The *ordering* is physically enforced by carrier speed — and our sense of a temporal gap between flash and thunder is precisely an information-arrival gap, not a physical separation of the event from itself. The cycle reads the same story twice, at two carrier speeds.\n- Astronomical illustration: the Sun is seen as it was 8.3 minutes ago; Andromeda as it was 2.5 million years ago. Star maps are not images of a single \"now\" — they are a montage of *delivered pasts*. The night sky is the cycle's canonical artifact."
       },
       "everyone": {
        "kind": "list",
        "text": "- **Physical ordering:** arrivals come in sequence $t_i = t_{0i} + d_i/c$.\n- **Perceptual integration:** the brain samples that ordered stream at its processing rate $I(t)$ and banks it in the felt-now window.\n- **Felt rate:** $\\frac{d\\tau}{dT} = \\Phi[I(t)]$.\n- **The everyday proof at dinner parties:** lightning, then thunder. Light arrives almost instantly; the sound wave lags about 3 seconds per kilometer of distance. You see the flash \"now\" and hear the thunder \"now-plus-delay\" — *the same event*, read twice at two carrier speeds. Your sense of a gap between flash and thunder is precisely an information-arrival gap, not the event separated from itself. The cycle reads the same story twice; the second telling is just slower.\n- **The astronomical version:** the Sun is seen as it was 8.3 minutes ago; Andromeda as it was 2.5 million years ago. A star map is not a photo of one \"now\" — it's a montage of *delivered pasts*. The night sky is the Light–Reality Cycle showing off.",
        "level": null,
        "number": null,
        "name": "- **Physical ordering:** arrivals come in sequence $t_i = t_{0i} + d_i/c$.\n- **Perceptual integration:** the brain samples that ordered stream at its processing rate $I(t)$ and banks it in the felt-now window.\n- **Felt rate:** $\\frac{d\\tau}{dT} = \\Phi[I(t)]$.\n- **The everyday proof at dinner parties:** lightning, then thunder. Light arrives almost instantly; the sound wave lags about 3 seconds per kilometer of distance. You see the flash \"now\" and hear the thunder \"now-plus-delay\" — *the same event*, read twice at two carrier speeds. Your sense of a gap between flash and thunder is precisely an information-arrival gap, not the event separated from itself. The cycle reads the same story twice; the second telling is just slower.\n- **The astronomical version:** the Sun is seen as it was 8.3 minutes ago; Andromeda as it was 2.5 million years ago. A star map is not a photo of one \"now\" — it's a montage of *delivered pasts*. The night sky is the Light–Reality Cycle showing off."
       }
      }
     ]
